> Build this as a **policy layer for deliberative routing and configuration**, not merely a model router.
> 
> Its job is:
> 
> > Given a task context, candidate models, tools, and execution budgets, choose an execution plan that maximizes expected *trustworthy task utility*, subject to cost and latency constraints; then learn from feedback and evaluation.
> 
> That framing keeps it reusable beyond chat and thumbs.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Separate the concepts that are easy to conflate
> 
> A thumbs-up is useful, but it is not the same thing as response quality. Your system should explicitly keep these quantities separate.
> 
> For an execution plan $p$—e.g., “Gemini Flash + retrieve 8 memory items + no web”, or “OpenAI reasoning model + web search + citation verifier”—estimate:
> 
> | Quantity | Meaning | Main evidence |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Preference / satisfaction** | Will James (or another user) like this answer? | Thumbs, edits, follow-up behavior |
> | **Task quality** | Is the response correct, complete, useful, and well reasoned? | Rubrics, tests, judge models, trusted-source checks, human audits |
> | **Reliability / safety** | How likely is it to make unsupported claims or fail badly? | Calibration tests, factuality checks, outcome audits |
> | **Cost** | Expected marginal dollar/token/tool cost | Observed logs |
> | **Latency** | Expected response time, including tool calls | Observed logs |
> | **Uncertainty** | How poorly do we know the above for this context? | Data coverage, model confidence, evaluation disagreement |
> 
> Then your policy can optimize a utility such as:
> 
> \[
> U(p \mid x)
> =
> w_s \hat S(p,x)
> +
> w_q \hat Q(p,x)
> -
> w_c \hat C(p,x)
> -
> w_l \hat L(p,x)
> -
> w_r \hat R(p,x)
> +
> w_u \hat U_{\mathrm{info}}(p,x)
> \]
> 
> where:
> 
> - $x$ is the task/context,
> - $\hat S$ is predicted user satisfaction,
> - $\hat Q$ is independent estimated quality,
> - $\hat R$ is expected failure risk,
> - and $\hat U_{\mathrm{info}}$ rewards selective exploration where uncertainty is high.
> 
> The key is that **thumb feedback updates $\hat S$ strongly, but should not be allowed to wholly define $\hat Q$ or $\hat R$**.
> 
> That is the basic defense against “cheap, fluent hallucination wins the thumbs metric.”
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Make the unit of selection an *execution plan*
> 
> Do not select only a model. Select a structured plan.
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class ExecutionPlan:
>     primary_model: ModelRef
>     deliberation_model: ModelRef | None = None
>     tools: list[ToolInvocation] = field(default_factory=list)
>     response_mode: str = "direct"  # direct, research, code, critique, etc.
>     verification: VerificationPolicy = VerificationPolicy.none()
>     budgets: Budgets = Budgets(
>         max_cost_usd=0.05,
>         max_latency_s=8,
>         max_tool_calls=4,
>     )
> ```
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```python
> ExecutionPlan(
>     primary_model="openai:gpt-5-mini",
>     tools=[
>         ToolInvocation(
>             name="memory.retrieve",
>             config={
>                 "collections": ["preferences", "project_state"],
>                 "top_k": 6,
>                 "reranker": "cross_encoder_v2",
>                 "min_score": 0.58,
>             },
>         )
>     ],
>     verification=VerificationPolicy(
>         mode="claim_check_if_external_facts",
>         threshold=0.65,
>     ),
> )
> ```
> 
> This matters because tool *configuration* is part of the policy:
> 
> - retrieval collections and filters;
> - $k$, score thresholds, reranker, token budget;
> - whether memory is injected directly, summarized, or cited internally;
> - web-search query count and source-quality filter;
> - code execution timeout and test strategy;
> - which model drafts, critiques, synthesizes, or verifies;
> - whether a second pass is warranted.
> 
> A model can be excellent but harmed by irrelevant memory; a modest model can be made strong on a narrow task by retrieval and verification. The policy should learn this at the plan level.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Define general interfaces so chat is just one adapter
> 
> Use a domain-neutral core whose inputs and outputs are structured.
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class Task:
>     task_id: str
>     input: Any                      # chat turn, document, API payload, etc.
>     modality: str                   # "chat", "batch", "agent_step", ...
>     context: Context
>     constraints: Constraints
>     objective: ObjectiveSpec
> 
> @dataclass
> class Context:
>     conversation_summary: str | None
>     user_profile_refs: list[str]
>     task_features: dict[str, Any]
>     available_tools: list[ToolRef]
>     environment: dict[str, Any]
> 
> @dataclass
> class Outcome:
>     task_id: str
>     plan: ExecutionPlan
>     output: Any
>     traces: ExecutionTrace
>     measurements: Measurements
>     feedback: list[FeedbackEvent]
> ```
> 
> And make feedback extensible:
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class FeedbackEvent:
>     source: str             # user, evaluator, unit_test, production_metric...
>     signal_type: str        # thumbs, rating, correction, success, latency...
>     value: float | str | bool
>     confidence: float
>     metadata: dict
> ```
> 
> A chat thumbs-up becomes one event:
> 
> ```python
> FeedbackEvent(
>     source="user",
>     signal_type="thumb",
>     value=True,
>     confidence=1.0,
>     metadata={"turn_id": "..."}
> )
> ```
> 
> But so can:
> 
> - “the user copied this answer into their codebase”;
> - a test suite pass rate;
> - whether a generated citation resolves;
> - whether the user later corrected a factual claim;
> - expert review;
> - downstream workflow success;
> - an evaluator score using a task-specific rubric.
> 
> This supports chat first, without making chat feedback a hard architectural constraint.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Use a layered decision architecture
> 
> I would not start with one giant learned router. Begin with four separable components.
> 
> ### A. Task understanding / feature extraction
> 
> Infer features such as:
> 
> - task family: coding, factual research, creative writing, planning, personal continuity, numerical analysis, etc.;
> - stakes: low / medium / high;
> - freshness requirement;
> - factuality requirement;
> - need for private context or memory;
> - whether exactness, citations, or tool execution is needed;
> - input/output size;
> - user urgency and budget preference;
> - ambiguity / likely value of asking a clarifying question.
> 
> These features may initially be deterministic rules plus a lightweight classifier. Log them all.
> 
> ### B. Candidate-plan generator
> 
> Generate a small, diverse set of feasible plans rather than considering the full combinatorial model $\times$ tool configuration space.
> 
> Example candidate set:
> 
> 1. **Fast direct**: low-cost model, no tools.
> 2. **Contextual direct**: low-cost model + tightly scoped memory retrieval.
> 3. **Research**: capable model + web + citation verification.
> 4. **Code**: code-oriented model + sandbox + tests.
> 5. **High-assurance**: draft + independent critique/verification.
> 6. **Clarify first**: ask one focused question.
> 
> Use templates / plan families at first. This both makes debugging tractable and stops the learner discovering bizarre expensive pipelines.
> 
> ### C. Quality, cost, latency, and risk estimators
> 
> Each plan gets predictions conditioned on task features.
> 
> Initially these can be simple:
> 
> - rolling empirical statistics by task family;
> - vendor/model benchmarks;
> - manually maintained priors;
> - observed cost and latency distributions;
> - rule-based penalties, e.g. no-web plan on freshness-sensitive question;
> - tool applicability priors.
> 
> Over time, replace or augment them with learned models:
> 
> \[
> \hat Q = f_Q(x, p), \qquad
> \hat S = f_S(x,p), \qquad
> \hat C = f_C(x,p), \qquad
> \hat L = f_L(x,p), \qquad
> \hat R = f_R(x,p).
> \]
> 
> Crucially, these should return **distributions or intervals**, not just point estimates. Route cautiously when uncertainty is high.
> 
> ### D. Constrained policy / selector
> 
> Choose the plan maximizing utility, but enforce hard constraints before utility optimization:
> 
> ```python
> feasible = [
>     p for p in candidates
>     if p.expected_cost_p95 <= task.constraints.max_cost
>     and p.expected_latency_p95 <= task.constraints.max_latency
>     and p.risk_upper_bound <= task.constraints.max_risk
> ]
> chosen = max(feasible, key=lambda p: policy.utility(task, p))
> ```
> 
> For high-stakes tasks, do not let a cheap expected score override reliability:
> 
> \[
> \Pr(\mathrm{material\ failure} \mid x,p) < \epsilon
> \]
> 
> or require appropriate verification by policy.
> 
> This is much better than pricing risk as a soft term alone.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. Treat model/tool capability as a prior, continuously calibrated
> 
> Your concern about xAI is exactly right. Pure user feedback has selection bias and rewards surface fluency. It also may not reveal errors until much later.
> 
> Maintain a **capability registry** that records evidence independently of user thumbs:
> 
> ```yaml
> capability_profile:
>   provider: xai
>   model: grok-...
>   version: ...
>   task_family: factual_research
>   quality_prior:
>     mean: 0.68
>     uncertainty: 0.10
>   factuality:
>     hallucination_rate_estimate: 0.12
>     benchmark_source: internal_audit_2026q3
>   strengths:
>     - informal synthesis
>     - fast ideation
>   weaknesses:
>     - source-grounded factual claims
>   recommended_requirements:
>     - web_grounding
>     - citation_verification
> ```
> 
> Sources for the profile:
> 
> 1. **Offline evaluation suite** built from your actual work.
> 2. Public benchmarks, treated cautiously.
> 3. Vendor claims, treated more cautiously.
> 4. Production outcomes, corrected for selection bias.
> 5. Periodic human audit, especially on high-impact task types.
> 
> Your offline suite should be small at first but intentionally representative of your real use:
> 
> - R/Python coding and debugging;
> - climate/IAM analysis and source interpretation;
> - structured report/table production;
> - planning and decision analysis;
> - memory-grounded personal/project continuity;
> - creative family/D&D tasks;
> - web-fresh factual research;
> - adversarial cases where attractive hallucinations are tempting.
> 
> For each task, write a rubric, expected properties, and—where possible—objective tests. Version the suite. Run it when you add or change models, prompts, tools, retrieval configurations, or policy logic.
> 
> This becomes the system’s epistemic backbone.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 6. Learn from feedback with contextual bandits, but only after a strong baseline
> 
> The natural online-learning formulation is a **constrained contextual bandit**:
> 
> - context: task and user/context features $x$;
> - action: an execution plan $p$;
> - reward: a composite, delayed, noisy outcome;
> - constraints: cost, latency, reliability.
> 
> But do **not** jump immediately to fully online reinforcement learning. Start with a transparent policy and logging. Then use bandit-style learning to tune choices within safe plan families.
> 
> A practical progression:
> 
> ### Phase 1 — Rules + measurement
> 
> Hand-write initial routing policies:
> 
> - use memory retrieval for requests involving prior decisions, user preferences, projects, or continuation;
> - use web only where freshness/external verification matters;
> - use code
