> 
> Then the chat API can stay very small:
> 
> ```python
> planner = Planner(conversation_id="conv_123")
> 
> response = await planner.chat_response(
>     messages,
>     max_cost_usd=0.10,
>     max_latency_s=8,
> )
> 
> print(response.id)
> print(response.text)
> print(response.status)  # "final" or "provisional"
> ```
> 
> `chat_response()` returns the best answer available within the latency budget. If it has started useful background work—slow research, verification, image generation, etc.—it may return a `provisional` response.
> 
> Polling returns completed asynchronous updates, if any:
> 
> ```python
> updates = await planner.poll()
> 
> for update in updates:
>     print(update.id)
>     print(update.text)
>     print(update.reply_to_response_id)
>     print(update.kind)       # "research_update", "correction", "image", ...
>     print(update.artifacts)  # optional URLs/attachments/structured outputs
> ```
> 
> For the webpage, poll every few seconds while the conversation is open. Completed work is persisted against `conversation_id`, so a reload or later visit does not lose it.
> 
> Feedback remains equally simple:
> 
> ```python
> planner.vote_up(response.id)
> planner.vote_down(response.id, reason="incorrect")
> 
> # Optional richer signals later:
> planner.feedback(
>     response.id,
>     kind="correction",
>     value="The cited paper was not actually a review article.",
> )
> ```
> 
> The one additional method I would include immediately is inspection/debugging:
> 
> ```python
> planner.explain(response.id)
> # {
> #   "model": "gemini-2.5-flash",
> #   "tools": [{"name": "scholar_search", "config": {"max_results": 5}}],
> #   "estimated_cost_usd": 0.031,
> #   "estimated_latency_s": 4.2,
> #   "reason": "fresh, source-sensitive research question"
> # }
> ```
> 
> Internally, `chat_response()` chooses and launches a plan; `poll()` merely retrieves newly completed, user-deliverable results. That keeps the interface simple while leaving room for sophisticated asynchronous planning later.
